# Keep the app out of search results.
#
# app.iqrra.com is a signed-in tool, not an audience page. It has nothing to
# rank for, and it cannot rank well anyway: every route serves the same empty
# Expo shell — same <title>, no description, no content until the bundle boots.
#
# It was worse than useless before this file. The SPA catch-all in _redirects
# answers 200 to EVERY path, existing or not, so /anything-at-all returned a
# page instead of a 404 — an unbounded set of identical thin URLs, all of them
# crawlable, all reachable from the three followed links on iqrra.com. Google's
# quality assessment is site-wide, so that sprawl is a drag on the one page
# that is actually trying to rank.
#
# A header, not robots.txt, and the difference matters: Disallow stops the
# crawl, and a crawler that never fetches the page never reads the noindex —
# so disallowed URLs linked from elsewhere stay in the index, listed with no
# title. To REMOVE pages you must let Googlebot in and hand it this header.
# public/robots.txt therefore allows everything on purpose.
#
# Cloudflare Pages reads _headers from the root of what it serves;
# `expo export --platform web` copies public/ verbatim into dist/, which is
# why this sits beside _redirects rather than in some build config.

# Clickjacking, for the same single rule below: app.iqrra.com is a signed-in
# tool and nothing frames it. There is no <iframe> and no WebView anywhere in
# the app — the only two WebView mentions in the codebase are comments saying
# it deliberately carries no such dependency — so the strict answer is the
# correct one, and nobody can overlay an invisible copy of a teacher's session
# to harvest clicks on whatever sits under the cursor, «حذف الحساب» included.
#
# frame-ancestors only, deliberately NOT a full CSP. A script-src/style-src
# policy over a React Native Web bundle is worth doing and is a separate
# change: Expo emits inline styles, and the bundle's own eval-shaped paths
# would need auditing first. A CSP that breaks the app is worse than no CSP.
# This directive governs who may embed the page and says nothing about what
# the page may load, so it cannot break rendering.
#
# Both spellings, because they are not the same coverage: frame-ancestors is
# the modern one, X-Frame-Options is what older browsers still honour.
#
# The four below are the rest of the baseline, and they are here for the same
# reason frame-ancestors is: none of them can break a React Native Web bundle,
# so the only thing that kept them out was nobody adding them.
#
# nosniff: the app serves its own JSON and fonts, and a browser that
# second-guesses a declared type is a browser that can be talked into treating
# one as script.
#
# HSTS: app.iqrra.com is HTTPS-only already. This closes the first plaintext
# request, the one that happens before a redirect can say otherwise. No
# includeSubDomains — iqrra.com's other names live in a different repo (see
# the landing site) and this file has no authority over them.
#
# Referrer-Policy: URLs here carry class and evaluation ids. strict-origin-
# when-cross-origin still sends the full path to our own API, and only the
# bare origin to anyone else.
#
# Permissions-Policy: this app asks for a microphone (read-aloud practice) and
# nothing else. Denying the rest costs nothing and means an embedded third
# party cannot ask on the app's behalf.
/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), microphone=(self)

# Cache the content-hashed assets forever.
#
# Cloudflare Pages defaults everything it serves to
# `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`. That is right for index.html, whose
# name stays the same while its bytes change, and wrong for every path below,
# whose filename contains a hash of its own contents. Those cannot go stale: a
# changed byte produces a changed filename, so a cached copy is either correct
# or unreferenced.
#
# Measured on 2026-09-15, before this: each asset cost a revalidation round
# trip on every page load — 304, empty body, ~0.19s. ETags were doing their
# job and the bytes were not re-sent; the latency was pure waste, paid once
# per asset per load, on connections where latency is the expensive part.
#
# These add only Cache-Control. The /* block above sets none, so nothing here
# overrides it — the noindex and frame-ancestors headers still apply to these
# paths too.
#
# DO NOT add a rule for / or /index.html. Its name is stable across deploys,
# so caching it immutably would pin browsers to whichever bundle they first
# loaded and no deploy would ever reach them — its must-revalidate default is
# exactly what makes a deploy visible. Same for /manifest.webmanifest,
# /favicon.ico, /robots.txt and /icons/*: stable names, deliberately absent.

# Metro's JS, CSS and fonts, e.g.
#   /_expo/static/js/web/entry-33d3af18603401295f8697900dc673d1.js
/_expo/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Bundled assets, including the ~1,500 book figures under
# assets/__knowledge-base/, e.g.
#   /assets/__knowledge-base/.../p066.39cdc03c3c6288c8bd8a9b090880e085.png
# The heaviest files the app serves — one figure measured 2.85MB — so a saved
# round trip matters most here.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
